  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nAn exceptionally quiet morning is ongoing across the area as a weak \ncold front pushed through, with winds turning westerly to \nnorthwesterly with clearing skies due to weak subsidence via CAA. \nThere is a bit of patchy fog within central MN, however it is not \nwidespread and we do not expect significant impacts as a result, \nwith fog quick to burn off once the sun rises in a few hours. \nTemperatures will remain a few degrees above normal today with highs \nin the low to mid 50s with weak surface high pressure centered south \nof the region keeping skies mostly clear. A few clouds return \ntonight as yet another weak Canadian low pressure system slides west \nto east across our neighbor to the north, with a similar situation \nto what we just had as a weak series of frontal boundaries will move \nthrough within 12 hours of each other between Monday evening and \nTuesday morning. No rain showers are expected with the main result \nbeing a brief increase in cloud cover overnight, with weak high \npressure once again sliding in afterwards on Tuesday. \n\nA ridge building in the western CONUS will amplify the northerly \nflow aloft on Wednesday which will also begin to spin up a weak \nsurface low tracking from roughly Nebraska to Illinois during the \nday as 500mb heights fall with northerly flow aloft. The height fall \nplus CAA could squeeze out a few showers mainly Wednesday morning as \nthe surface low moves south of the area, however the lack of \nmoisture should mean any activity is limited both in duration and \nintensity, with only a few hundredths of an inch at best from any \nshowers. As the surface low moves away from the area and heights \nbegin to steady later in the day, we should dry out quickly with \ncloud cover also retreating amidst high pressure building on the \neastern side of the ridge which has yet to really move from the \nwestern CONUS. The position of the ridge and high pressure will \nresult in some cooler temperatures from Wednesday through Friday \nwith another chance for some sub-freezing lows, mainly on Friday as \nhighs drop into the low to mid 40s. The high pressure then begins to \nmove towards the southeastern CONUS with the ridge beginning to \nflatten, raising 500mb heights and once again bringing temperatures \nback above normal, in addition to keeping us dry. This longwave \nridge spanning the entire CONUS looks to remain in place for at \nleast the first half of next week, with any potential pattern shift \nwaiting until the latter half of next week. Long range ensemble \nguidance keeps the weather quiet until then, however once the ridge \nmoves eastwards we could see better chances for precipitation return \nacross the area, but any major systems are too low confidence to \ntruly take note of for now.",
